  "question": {
    "text": "For $0 \\leq p \\leq 1$ and for any positive $a, b$, let $I(p)=(a+b)^{p}, J(p)=a^{P}+b^{P},$ then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "PF0oF",
      "text": "$l(p)>J(p)$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Vvw0D",
      "text": "$l(p) \\leq J(p)$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "OldOq",
      "text": "$l(p)    < J{(} p)$ in $\\left[0 ,\\frac{p}{2}\\right]$ and $l(p)>J(p)$ in $\\left[\\frac{p}{2}, \\infty\\right)$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uhOPC",
      "text": "$l(p)    < J(p)$ in $\\left[\\frac{p}{2}, \\infty\\right)$ and $J(p) < l(p)$ in $\\left[0, \\frac{p}{2}\\right]$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Here, let $p=\\frac{1}{m}$\r\nthen $\\left(a^{p}+b^{p}\\right)^{1 / p}=\\left(a^{1 / m}+b^{1 / m}\\right)^{m}$\r\n$=a+b+k, k \\geq 0$\r\n$\\therefore \\quad a^{p}+b^{p} \\geq(a+b)^{p} \\geq(a+b)$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad J(p) \\geq I(p)$",
    "image": null
